## Where the model cannot follow: bound-saturated days

On 16 days (2020-03-13 to 2020-04-08, the March-April 2020 crash) at least one variance level of the walk-forward fit sits on its upper bound (0.30, about 55% volatility),
so the model-implied 30-day vol is capped (jump model 52.7-68.6%, diffusion 52.6-70.2% on those days, while VIX on them ranged 51.5-83.6) and stops responding. The bottom rows of the statistics table repeat the jump-model and diffusion rows without those days.

## Source of the VIX data

`data/vix/india_vix.csv`, fetched by `fetch_vix.py` from NSE's own daily index-close archive (`nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/indices/ind_close_all_DDMMYYYY.csv`, the 'India VIX' row of each trading day: open, high, low, close; the 'Nifty 50' close is kept too). Yahoo's `^INDIAVIX` chart endpoint answered HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) on every attempt from this network, and niftyindices.com's historical-data endpoint returned an error, so the NSE archive was used; it is the exchange's own record. Fetch record:

```
source: NSE daily index-close archive
rows: 2470
first: 2016-10-03
last: 2026-10-06
fetched: 2026-10-08T12:22:03
earlier sources that failed: none
```

## Reading it honestly

- The comparison is with a model-free number on the same day. The model-implied vols are fitted to the same-day option prices (filtered to traded OTM quotes within |ln K/F| <= 0.22 and priced at least Rs 1), so a high co-movement says the fit tracks the market's volatility level, not that the model forecasts VIX.
- The model-implied number is a risk-neutral expectation of a mean-reverting variance; VIX's formula weights the whole strip including far wings that the filtered quotes do not cover, so a level gap of a few vol points is built in.
- Jump contribution: the three jump-variance choices give the same ordering; see `vix_vs_model_stats.csv` for all.

